## 2. Apache Server Setup (Rocky Linux)

### Install and Start Apache

```bash
sudo dnf install httpd openldap-clients nmap-ncat -y
sudo systemctl enable --now httpd
httpd -M | grep ldap
```

### Configure LDAP Hostname Resolution

Edit `/etc/hosts`:

```bash
sudo vi /etc/hosts
```

Add:

```text
192.168.56.109 ldap.mcloud.local
```

Verify connectivity:

```bash
getent hosts ldap.mcloud.local
nc -zv ldap.mcloud.local 389
```

### Create the Website Directory and Test Page

```bash
sudo mkdir -p /var/www/html/dl-mcloud-local
echo "LDAP Apache Test" | sudo tee /var/www/html/dl-mcloud-local/index.html
```

### Configure Apache LDAP Authentication

Create `/etc/httpd/conf.d/ldap.conf`:

```apache
<VirtualHost 192.168.56.111:80>
    DocumentRoot "/var/www/html/dl-mcloud-local"

    <Directory "/var/www/html/dl-mcloud-local">
        Options -Indexes +FollowSymLinks
        AllowOverride None

        AuthType Basic
        AuthName "LDAP Authentication"
        AuthBasicProvider ldap

        AuthLDAPURL "ldap://ldap.mcloud.local:389/dc=mcloud,dc=local?uid?sub?(objectClass=person)"

        AuthLDAPBindDN "cn=admin,dc=mcloud,dc=local"
        AuthLDAPBindPassword "REPLACE_WITH_ADMIN_PASSWORD"

        Require valid-user
    </Directory>

    ErrorLog /var/log/httpd/dl-mcloud-local_error.log
    CustomLog /var/log/httpd/dl-mcloud-local_access.log combined
</VirtualHost>
```

Replace `REPLACE_WITH_ADMIN_PASSWORD` with the actual LDAP admin password on the server. Never publish the real password.

### Test and Reload Apache

```bash
sudo apachectl -t
sudo httpd -S
sudo systemctl reload httpd
sudo systemctl status httpd
```

## 3. Test Authentication

Open in a browser:

```text
http://192.168.56.111
```

Log in with:

* Username: `fatemeh`
* Password: the LDAP user's password

Or test from the Apache server:

```bash
curl -I -u fatemeh http://192.168.56.111
```

The command prompts for the user's password. A successful authentication should normally return `HTTP/1.1 200 OK`.

